WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.433
Action.

2
00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:07.167
What the agent is allowed to do decides what it can learn, and which algorithms you can even use.

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:12.067
The action space is the agent's menu of choices in each state.

4
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:17.433
Discrete means a finite menu, like up, down, left, right, or buy, hold, sell.

5
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:22.200
Continuous means real numbers, like a steering angle or a thrust vector.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.367
Here is how the two flavors differ.

7
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:27.353
A discrete menu is finite.

8
00:00:27.353 --> 00:00:30.400
A continuous knob has infinitely many settings.

9
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.955
Q-learning needs a max over actions.

10
00:00:32.955 --> 00:00:36.400
With a continuous knob, that max stops being an option.

11
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:44.467
So discrete suits Q-tables and DQN, while continuous needs policy-gradient methods like PPO or SAC.

12
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:48.200
Those methods output a distribution over actions instead.

13
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.933
Menu design matters too.

14
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:54.600
Too coarse, and the best behavior may not even be expressible.

15
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.372
Too fine, and exploration takes forever.

16
00:00:57.372 --> 00:01:02.933
A probe with three hundred sixty headings explores ninety times slower than one with four.

17
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.367
Even the edges are a design decision.

18
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.669
What happens when the agent drives into a wall?

19
00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:12.800
Here it stays put and pays a small extra penalty.

20
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.955
Otherwise it makes a normal move.

21
00:01:14.955 --> 00:01:17.933
The learner will faithfully learn whatever you chose.

22
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:22.623
Same world, two designs: four discrete moves train in seconds.

23
00:01:22.623 --> 00:01:26.233
A continuous thrust needs PPO and far more samples.

24
00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:29.600
Say you're simulating a tumbling cubesat.

25
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:36.233
Three discrete actions, fire left, fire right, or coast, trains with DQN in minutes on a laptop.

26
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.667
A continuous torque vector needs PPO and hours.

27
00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:46.400
So start discrete to prove the pipeline works, and go continuous only if the coarse menu can't steady the spin.

28
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.486
Let's recap.

29
00:01:48.486 --> 00:01:51.367
A discrete menu suits Q-tables and DQN.

30
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.800
A continuous knob needs PPO or SAC.

31
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.667
The design of the menu shapes what is learnable.

32
00:01:57.967 --> 00:02:01.544
And edge behavior is your choice, so make it on purpose.

33
00:02:01.544 --> 00:02:02.567
Next up: reward.
